TASK-185 runtime harness: the Peek's Open in Split View, the normal-section
split from a favourite or pinned tab, Option-click and the link menu's Open
Link in Split View. Temporary code, never committed to Detour/. Apply, build,
run, revert:

    git apply docs/harness/task185-harness.patch
    xcodegen generate
    xcodebuild -scheme Detour -configuration Debug build -derivedDataPath .derived
    # fixture pages (host, pinned, fav, split-a/-b, psplit-a/-b, peek1/2, target
    # .html, each a heading plus a big link #l to /target.html) in $SITE:
    python3 -m http.server 8185 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory "$SITE" &
    OUT="$PWD/.derived-logs/task185"; mkdir -p "$OUT"
    DETOUR_DATA_DIR=DetourVerify185 DETOUR_VERIFY_DIR="$OUT" \
        TASK185_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8185 \
        .derived/Build/Products/Debug/Detour.app/Contents/MacOS/Detour &
    # wait for the "done" line in $OUT/verify.log (about 105 s), then kill that
    # pid only. A watcher runs `screencapture -x -o -l <windowNumber>` for each
    # "SHOOT <name> <windowNumber>" line.
    git apply -R docs/harness/task185-harness.patch && xcodegen generate
    rm -r -- ~/Library/Application\ Support/DetourVerify185

Needs an unlocked screen (posted clicks and the real context menu). Seeds a
normal tab, a lone pinned tab, a favourite, a normal split and a pinned split
from local pages, then, each followed by its undo:
1. Peek on the normal tab, navigated once inside the Peek, then
   `openPeekInSplitView`: host + new right pane, the right pane's web view `===`
   the Peek's, `canGoBack` kept, the pane selected and first responder, both
   panes hosted in the NSSplitView.
2. The same on the pinned tab and on the favourite: a split at the top of the
   normal section, a copy of the host on the left, the entry / favourite
   unchanged (id, URL, sort order, folder, group, backing tab).
3. Peek on a pane of the normal split and of the pinned split: no split button.
4. Option-click as a real posted left click with the Option flag on the page's
   link (so through `decidePolicy`), in the favourite, the lone pinned tab, a
   normal tab and a split pane.
5. The link menu as a real posted right click: WebKit builds the menu, a hook
   at the end of `BrowserWebView.willOpenMenu` checks the item (after Open Link
   in New Window; action, target, the WebKit item as representedObject) and
   performs it while the menu is up, so the URL arrives through WebKit's own
   "Open Link in New Window" and `createWebViewWith`. In the favourite, a normal
   tab and a split pane; plus a synthetic menu on a Peek's web view.
6. A second window on the same space selecting the host, then window A hands
   its Peek to a split.

Results (2026-10-24, run1, screen unlocked): 70 checks, 0 failures.
- Peek → split on the normal host: the web view kept (back list [peek1] at
  peek2), right pane selected and first responder, both panes in the split
  view; undo removed the pane, archived nothing, ungrouped the host.
- Pinned and favourite hosts: [copy, Peek page] as the first two normal tabs,
  the copy at the host's URL, the entry and the favourite unchanged before and
  after undo; undo left three tabs and no closed-tab records.
- Split hosts (normal and pinned): the overlay showed close and expand only.
- Option-click (real event): the favourite and the lone pinned tab opened
  [copy, target] at the top of the normal section with the right pane
  selected; a normal tab got its right pane; a split pane sent the link to
  the other pane.
- Link menu (real menu): items read New Tab, New Window, Split View, Download
  Linked File, ...; the item's routes matched Option-click for the favourite,
  a normal tab and a split pane; a Peek's menu has no split item.
- Two windows: both panes' containers stayed in window A, window B kept a
  snapshot (an NSImageView, no split view or container). B's snapshot is the
  one it took before the split formed (full-bleed host): a window already on
  a snapshot does not refresh it on a later hand-off, as for any split formed
  in another window.
Captured: the Peek with three buttons, each split, each undo, the Peeks on
split hosts with two buttons, window B. Not driven: a person's click on the
button itself (`openPeekInSplitView` was called directly; the button's
`onSplit` is the only caller), and the glide animation frame by frame.

